<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokhtamysh–Timur war</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a>–<a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> war was fought from 1386 to 1395 between Tokhtamysh, khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and his former patron Timur. Tokhtamysh raided Timur's lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, but Timur defeated him at the Kondurcha River (1391) and the Terek (1395) and then sacked the Horde's cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, a blow from which the Golden Horde never fully recovered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh-timur-war.jpg" alt="Tokhtamysh–Timur war" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 15th-century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_between_Timur_and_Toqtamish_Khan,_1420-1440,_possibly_Herat._Topkapi_H.2153.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of the succession crises among the Jochid rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. In the late 1370s and early 1380s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> take power in the White Horde from his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urus-khan">Urus Khan</a>. Tokhtamysh then seized the Blue Horde from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a> in 1380, and united the two hordes. He restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> power over the Russian principalities with a punitive campaign in 1381 and 1382, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> was besieged and sacked, but his ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> set him against Timur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> began his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 1383 and in 1385 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. In the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and in 1386 he plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and withdrew with rich booty. Between 1389 and 1391 the two sides fought hard, and Timur won the first phase at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kondurcha-river">Battle of the Kondurcha River</a>. Vasily I of Moscow used the war to his advantage: visiting the weakened khan's court in 1392, he was allowed to take the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> recovered, and in the spring of 1395 he raided the Timurid territory of Shirvan. Timur answered with a final assault and routed him at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek River</a> on 15 April 1395. He then destroyed the major cities of the khanate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, Ukek, Majar, Azaq, Tana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, and with them the Italian trading colonies of Sarai, Tana and Astrakhan, his first Western European victims. The Genoese city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> was spared.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> was deposed in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> and fled to the Ukrainian steppes, seeking help from Grand Duke Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Timur turned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> but withdrew from Russian territory, which chroniclers explained by a vision of the Virgin defending the city. Tokhtamysh and Vytautas were crushed by Temür Qutlugh and Edigu at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a> in 1399, and around 1406 Tokhtamysh was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> by Edigu's men.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> never recovered, and in the middle of the 15th century it broke up into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, Crimean, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, Qasim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> and Kazakh khanates and the Nogai Horde. Its weakness allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to continue the gathering of the Russian lands, although an invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> in 1408 led Vasily I to recognise the khan as his suzerain again in 1412. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship was finally shaken off at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a> in 1480.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokhtamysh–Timur war” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the last major battle of the <b>Tokhtamysh–Timur war</b>, Timur defeated Tokhtamysh, khan of the Golden Horde, at the Terek River in the North Caucasus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
